0328 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530328 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530328 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530328 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90762acc-754a-5149-93b4-5bc79b9c05e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives two lines\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20cfcb32-46de-59b7-8990-d20e7a58a53a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530432 #edb/ref 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ the level curves are $y=\\pm 2x,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=1$ the level curve is $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/63/634a863f2154398762af17da40b22d303b10b8846c90fa2021148779e1d1fa97.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=2{x}^{2}$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "248c7282-1f4b-5be4-93d4-cb424b9138b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530438 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =c \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{c}{2}}$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/84/844b92278c8b945a68104629b8dcb14d1b41c6875677ffda4da9615363e55221.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11b06b48-ff25-5bd7-85cb-a0e8a933ee28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186262934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530562 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a044a37-56f7-5838-a701-158e323361de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186338935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530564 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530564 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aee806c6-6654-5513-b824-ce14576c7eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530564 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c004fa3e-a13e-59b0-a5ae-8941190beebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186379910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df089dae-31df-5d21-8ad0-f21436e8ebd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530432 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
